INT. ICE FISHING HUT ON FROZEN LAKE - DAY

A shack the size of a closet. Bare wood benches. A hole in the floor with black water and a bobbing line. A propane heater ticks.

WENDELL "PA" BRODSKY, sixties, in a quilted vest, sits hunched over the hole with a short rod. His grandson TOBY, ten, in a puffy snowsuit, kneels across from him holding a thermos he can barely lift. RAYLENE "RAY" BRODSKY, sixteen, plugs one ear with an earbud and sulks on the far bench.

                    TOBY
          Pa, is it a bite?

                    WENDELL
          Not yet, bud.

                    TOBY
          How do you know?

                    WENDELL
          The line would twitch.

Toby stares at the line. It floats. He unscrews the thermos, pours cocoa onto his mitten, and licks it. Ray rolls her eyes and turns up her music.

                    RAY
          This is so boring.

                    WENDELL
          It's supposed to be.

                    RAY
          Then why do it?

                    WENDELL
          Because it's quiet.

                    RAY
          It's not quiet. Toby's slurping.

                    TOBY
          I'm not slurping.

                    RAY
          You are slurping.

Wendell lays a hand on the rod. He does not look at either of them. Toby slides closer and pushes the thermos into his grandfather's lap.

                    TOBY
          Pa, have some.

                    WENDELL
          Thank you, bud.

He sips. Wipes his mustache with the back of his glove. The heater ticks. A breath of wind pushes at the door, and the hut shivers.

                    RAY
          Pa, how long did Grandma fish with you?

                    WENDELL
          Forty years.

                    RAY
          Did she think it was boring?

                    WENDELL
          Every winter.

                    RAY
          Then why'd she come?

                    WENDELL
          Because I did.

Ray lowers the earbud. She looks at the hole in the floor, the black circle, and the line sitting in the middle of it like a thin white thread.

                    TOBY
          Pa. Pa! It twitched.

                    WENDELL
          Hold on, bud.

                    TOBY
          It did! I saw!

Ray slides off her bench onto her knees beside him and Toby, and the three of them lean over the hole, nearly touching heads. The line dips once. Then goes still.

                    RAY
          False alarm.

                    TOBY
          It twitched.

                    WENDELL
          It's thinking about it.

They wait. The heater ticks. Toby's head sinks onto Wendell's shoulder. Ray lets the earbud dangle.

                    RAY
          Pa?

                    WENDELL
          Mm.

                    RAY
          This isn't that boring.

He doesn't answer. He just watches the line, and the corner of his mustache moves.
